/// Returns whether the `min`/`max` statistics can be used to derive a *sound* skip-batch predicate
/// for a comparison of a column of type `dtype` against a literal `lv` using operator `op`.
///
/// The `op` is expressed as if the column is on the left-hand side (e.g. `col(A) < lv`). For
/// range checks like `is_between` where there is no single binary operator, `op` is `None` and the
/// literal must simply be a usable (non-NaN) bound.
///
/// Floating point columns need special care. Parquet statistics *exclude* `NaN` values, but the
/// data may still contain them, and under `TotalOrd` `NaN` is the largest value. Concretely:
/// - `col < x` / `col <= x` are always safe: a hidden `NaN` never satisfies them.
/// - `col > x` / `col >= x` are unsafe for non-`NaN` `x`: a hidden `NaN` would satisfy them but is
///   not reflected in the `max` statistic.
/// - `col > NaN` is safe: nothing is greater than `NaN`.
/// - `col >= NaN` is unsafe: `NaN >= NaN` holds, but `NaN` is excluded from the `max` statistic so
///   `max < NaN` cannot detect it.
/// - `col == x` is safe for non-`NaN` `x` (`NaN` never equals a non-`NaN` value), but `col == NaN`
///   is unsafe because the statistics exclude `NaN`.
/// - `col != x` is never safe: a hidden `NaN` always satisfies it.
fn can_use_min_max_stats(dtype: &DataType, op: Option<Operator>, lv: Option<&LiteralValue>) -> bool {
    use Operator as O;

    // These types don't have a usable total order for `min`/`max` based skipping.
    if dtype.is_nested() || dtype.is_null() || dtype.is_categorical() {
        return false;
    }

    // Non-float types are always fine.
    if !dtype.is_float() {
        return true;
    }

    match op {
        Some(O::Lt) | Some(O::LtEq) => true,
        None | Some(O::Eq) | Some(O::EqValidity) => lv.is_some() && !lv_is_nan(lv),
        Some(O::Gt) => lv_is_nan(lv),
        _ => false,
    }
